WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.830
- Thanks to Backblaze for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air,

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:05.300
or, cloud, backed up in the cloud.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.390
President Trump uses his pardon power

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.040
and commutes Roger Stone's sentence.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.250
- I'm getting rave reviews for
what I did for Roger Stone.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.450
- Is there anything to be done?

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.770
Are there limits to this pardon power?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.900
As you may recall, Roger Stone is proud

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.550
to call himself a dirty trickster.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.560
Stone has been around since the Nixon era,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.010
but he found his true home

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.530
as an advisor to President Donald Trump.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.150
And last year, Stone was convicted

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.230
of obstruction of justice,
giving false statements

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.070
to Congress, and witness tampering

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.300
stemming from the findings

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.140
of Special Counsel Robert Mueller's probe

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.080
into Russian election interference.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.200
And last week, President Trump commuted

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.360
Roger Stone's three year sentence.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.210
This is in stark contrast
to the two individuals

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.010
who have offered testimony
against the president,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.630
Lieutenant Commander Alexander Vindman,

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.680
whose military career is now over,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.160
and Michael Cohen, who is now in prison.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.100
Roger Stone lied for the president,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.770
and now, theoretically,
at least, he is free.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.540
Did President Trump do anything
illegal in this instance?

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:00.490
And could legislators pass a law

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.510
to limit the president's power?

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.773
Well, let's find out.

00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:06.606
(dramatic music)

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.460
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.720
because at least one of
the president's confidants

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.930
is probably not going to jail.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.080
Now, before we talk about the legality

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.970
of the commutation of
Roger Stone's sentence,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.660
and what that actually means,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.030
let's talk about what
Roger Stone actually did

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.370
that theoretically was
going to send him to jail

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:28.380
in the first place.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.510
Now, a lot of Americans
still have the perception

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.430
that the Mueller investigation
was a political witch hunt.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.510
I can't imagine where they got that from,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.220
but it's worth recalling just
how important the inquiry was

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.130
into the 2016 election
and how Roger Stone's lies

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.880
helped protect the president.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.410
Now, a lot of this information
was already public record,

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.020
but a good portion of it was
just conveyed to Congress

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.290
by Aaron Zelinsky, the U.S. attorney

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.150
who prosecuted Roger Stone.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.860
You might recall that
Attorney General Barr

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.070
filed a memorandum effectively retracting

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:59.760
Zelinsky's recommendation
that Roger Stone serve

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.670
a serious prison term as a result

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.770
of the obstruction of
justice related charges,

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.810
and Zelinsky withdrew in protest,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.810
and then testified before Congress.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.570
But going back even further,
based on Zelinsky's testimony

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.960
and "The Mueller Report" itself,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.790
you might recall that in 2016
U.S. intelligence agencies

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.010
determined that Russian
President Vladimir Putin

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.810
wanted to damage Hillary
Clinton's election chances.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.070
Putin wanted to convey
damaging information

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.630
about the Clinton campaign
and wanted that information

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.120
to come from a source that had

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.030
the appearance of authenticity.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.670
So Putin selected WikiLeaks,
because of the perception

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.850
that WikiLeaks was a
righteous truth-teller,

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.330
rather than a front for any
government or political party.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.730
U.S. intelligence said that
WikiLeaks was being used

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.230
by Russia's GRU.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.010
That assessment was confirmed by other

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.480
Western intelligence agencies,
including the British.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.960
Now, during the 2016 election,
the Russian government

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.590
conspired with WikiLeaks
to influence U.S. elections

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.160
by hacking accounts belonging

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.210
to the Democratic National Committee,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.810
Hillary Clinton, and her
advisor, John Podesta.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.510
The computer attacks on the
Clinton campaign operatives

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.730
were in fact considered federal crimes,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.420
and that is one of the
indictments that came out

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.730
as a result of the Mueller investigation.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.490
Under federal law, it is
illegal to hack someone's email

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.890
and social media accounts,

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.240
and the Mueller investigation
led to the Russians

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.790
who were involved in the computer hacks

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.700
being indicted under federal crimes,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.490
though since they're not in the U.S.,

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.730
it's unlikely that they
will actually be prosecuted.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.390
And on June 14, 2016, the DNC announced

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.170
its servers had been
hacked, and that same day,

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.070
Roger Stone called Donald Trump

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.230
on Trump's personal telephone line.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.690
On August 2, 2016, Stone called Trump,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.740
and they chatted for 10 minutes.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.780
After that talk, Roger Stone then told

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.150
conspiracy theorist and
radio host Jerome Corsi

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.470
to get someone in London to
go talk to Julian Assange,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.620
the WikiLeaks co-founder,
who was then living

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.210
in the Embassy of Ecuador
while he fought extradition.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.930
Two days later on August
4th, Corsi sent Roger Stone

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.290
an email informing him
that Assange was planning

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.210
two more document releases
to damage Hillary Clinton.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.950
One of those releases would be in October

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.810
to have maximum impact
on the November election.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.267
Corsi told Stone that, quote,

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:03.940
"hackers" were working to make it happen.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:07.060
During August, the Trump
campaign staffer, Rick Gates,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.950
who later pleaded guilty
to lying to FBI agents,

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.430
had conversations with Stone

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.500
about the WikiLeaks document releases.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.780
Gates was also present
when Donald Trump had

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.350
a telephone call with Stone.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.220
After the call, Trump told Gates

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.990
more information was planned.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:22.490
Trump's lawyer, Michael Cohen,

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.550
also convicted of federal crimes,

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.550
heard Trump on the phone with Stone,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.310
who allegedly told Trump
what WikiLeaks was doing.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.580
Trump campaign manager Paul Manafort,

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.330
also convicted of federal crimes,

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.220
told investigators that
Donald Trump told him

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.630
to keep in touch with Stone

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.500
for more information about WikiLeaks.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.480
Now, despite Roger Stone's
links to Donald Trump,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.070
the president claimed
amnesia about whether he had

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.330
any conversations with Stone
during the summer of 2016.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.240
Shortly after telling Mueller's team

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.130
that he couldn't remember
any conversations

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.030
in written answers, Donald
Trump blasted witnesses

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.630
that he perceived as flipping on him,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.510
and Trump praised Stone for his bravery

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.327
in refusing to snitch on
Trump and the Trump campaign.

00:05:03.327 --> 00:05:05.520
"The Mueller Report"
noted that Trump's actions

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.270
towards Stone support an inference

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.180
that he was offering
Stone and other witnesses

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.020
rewards for not providing
damaging testimony,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.090
rewards like pardons.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.500
After Trump tweeted about Stone's bravery

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.640
and the treachery of snitches,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.410
Roger Stone lied to the
House Intelligence Committee.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.820
Those lies were particularly brazen.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.490
Roger Stone claimed he didn't have

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.400
anything in writing pertaining to Assange.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.270
In reality, he had hundreds of documents.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.370
He said he got the
information about WikiLeaks

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.580
from talk show host Randy Credico,

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.660
when actually he got the
information from Jerome Corsi,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.030
and perhaps vice versa.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.680
This predictably sent
investigators on a wild goose chase

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.720
looking for information from Credico.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.530
The real intermediary,
Corsi, never testified

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:46.750
before the House Intelligence Committee,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.490
because potentially Stone lied

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.080
and they didn't know
about Corsi's involvement.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.220
Roger Stone also said he
didn't have communications

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.910
with anyone from the Trump campaign,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.580
when he actually talked with Manafort,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.420
Gates, Bannon, and Trump.

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.060
And it turns out that Roger Stone also had

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.340
email conversations
with Corsi and Credico,

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.150
despite telling Congress that
he wasn't "an email guy."

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.300
Now, as a federal judge noted when Stone

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.420
was eventually sentenced, Stone obstructed

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.290
Congress' ability to know
what actually happened,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.930
and potentially led to the destruction

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:16.900
of much of the material Roger Stone

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:18.430
actually had in his possession.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.420
And Stone pressured Randy Credico

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.500
to go along with Stone's made-up stories.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:26.630
On April 9, 2018 in an email
chain about Stone's testimony,

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.087
Roger Stone wrote to Credico,

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.017
"I'm going to take that dog away from you.

00:06:30.017 --> 00:06:32.277
"Not a (bleep) thing you
can do about it either,

00:06:32.277 --> 00:06:35.370
"because you are a weak,
broke piece of (bleep),"

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.080
and Stone also told Credico
to, quote, "prepare to die."

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.170
Now, although Credico
said he did not believe

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:43.990
Stone would actually steal
his dog or hurt him directly,

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:46.240
he was worried that Stone's comments would

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.950
encourage others to
hurt him and his family.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:50.860
Stone even suggested that those testifying

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.850
regarding the WikiLeaks information should

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:55.730
pull a Frank Pentangeli
to prevent contradicting

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:57.370
his earlier testimony.

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.190
Pentangeli was a character
from "The Godfather Part II"

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.230
who lied to Congress about organized crime

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:05.110
in order to save Don Corleone
from a perjury charge.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:08.360
- I don't, I never knew no godfather.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.960
- Spoiler, the character later
slits his wrists in prison.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.790
Now, eventually some of
Stone's lies were discovered

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.520
and the DOJ prosecuted him.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.410
During Stone's trial,
the government admitted

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.650
for the first time that
Stone may have discussed

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:23.930
WikiLeaks with Donald Trump
during the summer of 2016.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.460
And Rick Gates testified
that he was in the limousine

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.860
with Trump on their way
to LaGuardia Airport

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.520
when Trump talked on the phone
with Stone about the emails.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.330
This information was apparently redacted

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:35.360
from "The Mueller Report,"

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.720
and was not made public
until the Stone trial.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:39.920
It directly contradicts
President Trump's statement

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.547
to Robert Mueller that, quote,

00:07:41.547 --> 00:07:44.187
"I do not recall discussing
WikiLeaks with Stone,

00:07:44.187 --> 00:07:47.117
"nor do I recall being aware
of Mr. Stone having discussed

00:07:47.117 --> 00:07:50.500
"WikiLeaks with individuals
associated with my campaign."

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:51.570
Of course, it's not clear,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.330
and we may never know as
to whether Stone was lying

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.340
to prevent the information of the fact

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.300
that the president had
discussed the WikiLeaks

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.640
email hacks before they were released,

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.430
or whether there was more information

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.190
that is not yet public that was even worse

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:07.810
than this particular scandal.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:11.020
And now, Roger Stone has
allegedly been rewarded

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.290
with a commutation of his sentence.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:14.570
Now, I say allegedly,

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:17.590
because there's some really
weird stuff going on,

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.000
and before we get into
the actual pardon power,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.320
and what that means, and
what the president can do

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.040
to commute someone's sentence,

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.060
and now questions have
arisen as to whether

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:27.700
President Trump's clemency covers

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.700
only Roger Stone's prison
time or also his probation,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:34.240
and the U.S. Probation
Office has raised questions

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.220
about the commutation itself.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:37.640
So, at the time of this video,

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.380
no one has actually seen
this supposed commutation

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:41.930
of Roger Stone's sentence,

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:44.590
and we don't actually know
exactly what it covers.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.740
Now, apart from that legal weirdness,

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.810
you'll also hear some
individuals refer to the crimes

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.290
that Roger Stone was convicted of as being

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.460
mere process crimes, and
therefore they're not

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.260
really particularly important crimes.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.780
In fact, Senator Lindsey
Graham has basically said

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.300
that he's fine with a pardon,

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.430
because Stone was merely
convicted of process crimes.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:07.230
Generally, a process crime in this context

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.420
is a pejorative used to say that the crime

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:14.410
only came about as a result
of the legal process itself.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.820
These include things like
perjury, obstruction of justice,

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.210
making false statements, and
failure to appear in court.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.930
Now, as a lawyer, I find
this particularly galling,

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:27.930
because process crimes can
undermine the rule of law

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.757
as much, if not more, than other, quote,

00:09:30.757 --> 00:09:34.030
"non-process crimes,"
because if you can get away

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:36.800
with mucking around with
the justice system itself,

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:39.320
the justice system will stop working.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.490
Why is lying to law
enforcement agents illegal

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:42.810
when a person isn't under oath?

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.620
Because it impedes the search
for evidence and the truth.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.300
When witnesses lie, they
obstruct the officer's ability

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.810
to find evidence.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.960
Obstruction of justice obstructs justice.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:54.650
It's right there in the title.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.670
It lets witnesses manipulate the process

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.860
in sending agents in the wrong direction

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:01.350
and enabling the destruction
of evidence itself.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.770
It's kind of hard to square
the idea of being in favor

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:07.700
of law and order if you
are not in favor of these,

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.290
quote, "process crimes,"
and thinking that people

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:13.290
should be able to get away with lying

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:14.820
to law enforcement agencies,

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.600
obstructing justice, and
intimidating witnesses.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:21.620
So let's talk about the formal
constitutional pardon power.

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.980
Donald Trump has long been
obsessed with pardon power.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.210
He has stated that he
more or less believes

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.160
that it gives him unlimited
constitutional authority

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.780
to do people favors.

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.440
He's even suggested that
he could pardon himself,

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.210
but what is the pardon power?

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.740
Is Donald Trump right that
it gives him total power

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.260
to commute and pardon
people for federal crimes?

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.220
Well, under Article II, Section
2 of the U.S. Constitution,

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:43.457
the president was given the, quote,

00:10:43.457 --> 00:10:45.757
"power to grant reprieves and pardons

00:10:45.757 --> 00:10:47.427
"for offenses against the United States,

00:10:47.427 --> 00:10:49.690
"except in cases of impeachment."

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:52.270
The Supreme Court case of
Ex parte Garland established

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:54.720
the contours of the
president's pardon authority.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.710
Garland involved a presidential pardon

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:58.360
given by President Andrew Johnson,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.430
who pardoned thousands
of Confederate soldiers

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.150
for their arguable crimes against America

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.850
in participating in the Civil War.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:07.610
However, Congress then passed
a law disbarring lawyers

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.830
who were members of the
Confederate government.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.100
Augustus Garland was a Confederate Senator

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.730
and a lawyer who claimed that the law

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:15.980
was a bill of attainder
and an ex post facto law

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.610
that punished him for a crime

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.800
that he had already been pardoned for.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:21.500
The Supreme Court ruled for Garland,

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.890
and this ruling established
two key principles.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:25.700
First, the president has full power

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.490
to pardon anyone of all federal crimes,

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:29.980
either before, during
or after prosecution.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.360
The pardon clears the
individuals of any consequences

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.750
that they may have arisen from the action

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:36.690
from which they were being punished.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.080
And second, laws may not
be established that would

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.790
punish someone after
the fact of the pardon.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.470
This prevents Congress from passing a law

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.890
punishing someone who the
president actually pardoned,

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.210
and commutations are another power granted

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:51.010
by Article II, Section 2,
and that's what the reprieve

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.010
referred to in the text refers to.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:55.590
Unlike pardons, which formally
forgive the individual

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.730
of the crimes and restore
what may have been lost

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:00.170
through the conviction,
a commutation just ends

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.600
the person's sentence.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.990
Roger Stone received a
commutation, at least, allegedly.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:06.960
In the White House statement
announcing the commutation,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.820
the White House indicated
that a commutation was chosen

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.650
rather than a pardon because
Stone does not concede

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:15.830
his guilt and will challenge
his conviction on appeal.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.020
Now, the commutation of
Stone's sentence has made

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:20.750
a lot of people mad on
both sides of the aisle,

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.300
even including Andrew McCarthy,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:23.670
who writes for the "National Review,"

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:26.300
which is incredibly conservative,
and incredibly pro-Trump.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:30.040
- He got caught up in a very
politicized investigation.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.770
- And when you lose Andrew McCarthy,

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.170
you can bet that your actions are

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.230
pretty far outside the Overton window.

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.380
So as a result, a lot
of people have started

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.450
talking about limits on the
president's pardon power.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:42.500
And in fact, there are some limits

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:43.940
on the president's powers.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:47.070
And since the constitutional
text only mentions reprieves

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.310
and pardons for offenses
against the United States,

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:51.000
the president can only pardon people

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.800
for federal criminal acts.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.990
People convicted of violating state law

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.550
can only seek clemency
from their governors.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.040
The pardon power also has
nothing to do with civil cases.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:03.000
The president cannot remove
federal civil liability

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.600
for things like damages.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.960
There's also a legal consensus

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.130
that the president cannot pardon himself

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.580
after he has been impeached and removed.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:12.740
Theoretically, the president
can only pardon someone

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.400
for crimes that have already occurred.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.570
In other words, the president
cannot pardon people

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:19.670
for a conspiracy that is
still in process and ongoing.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:21.730
This was established in Ex parte Garland

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:23.920
because the decision
specified that the power

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.710
may be exercised at any time

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.610
after the commission of a crime.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.140
We could get into a deep argument here

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:31.390
about whether Roger Stone's
crimes against the United States

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:33.330
are actually part of an ongoing conspiracy

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.700
with President Trump and others,

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.280
but that would probably
lead us back to the text

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:37.700
of Article II, Section 2.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.780
What does it mean that the
president's power to pardon

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.750
is limited by impeachment?

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:43.860
You might recall that the
pardon clause says that

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.487
the president has the
"power to grant reprieves

00:13:45.487 --> 00:13:47.727
"and pardons for offenses
against the United States,

00:13:47.727 --> 00:13:50.440
"except in cases of impeachment."

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:51.640
It simply says impeachment,

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.860
rather than impeachment and conviction.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:55.580
And traditionally, we have
assumed that the phrase

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.250
except in cases of impeachment means

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.890
that a president cannot use the pardon

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.550
to stop an impeachment.

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:02.790
This has been the prevailing
view for hundreds of years,

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:05.100
mostly due to a book written
by Supreme Court Justice

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.410
Joseph Story in 1833.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:09.020
But detractors of this theory point out

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.960
that Story did not have Madison's notes

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.520
from the Constitutional Convention,

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.790
which are considered some of
the most important sources

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:17.290
of information for what the
framers actually thought.

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:19.640
And Story's interpretation
gives significant weight

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.440
to the way that pardon power was used

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:22.840
in English common law.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.820
Under this view, President
Trump obviously could not

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:27.700
pardon himself to stop the
House from impeaching him.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:30.200
However, there are two
other interpretations

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.810
of this clause that have
become relevant recently.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.080
First, it could mean that
the president's pardon power

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.890
is suspended after the
House votes to impeach.

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:40.550
James Madison felt that a
strong Congress would realize

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.450
that the president was
conspiring to commit treason,

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:45.060
impeach, and remove him for
it, averting the crisis.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.657
Madison said, quote, "If
the president be connected

00:14:47.657 --> 00:14:50.117
"in any suspicious manner with any persons

00:14:50.117 --> 00:14:53.537
"and there be to believe
he will shelter himself,

00:14:53.537 --> 00:14:55.537
"the House of Representatives
can impeach him.

00:14:55.537 --> 00:14:58.397
"They can suspend him when suspected,

00:14:58.397 --> 00:15:00.867
"and the power of pardoning will devolve

00:15:00.867 --> 00:15:02.230
"to the vice-president."

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.150
Now, a second interpretation of the text,

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.000
also drawn from Madison's words,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.370
is that the president can't
pardon people connected

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.050
with the articles of impeachment.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.657
Here, the emphasis is on the
first part of Madison's quote,

00:15:12.657 --> 00:15:15.357
"If the president be connected
in any suspicious manner

00:15:15.357 --> 00:15:16.650
"with any persons."

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.380
So, does this mean that the president

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.640
cannot pardon people
with whom he conspired?

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.990
Well, while it's possible
that the Supreme Court

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:24.970
could interpret the
pardon clause that way,

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:27.240
odds are the Supreme
Court would probably say

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:30.230
that the president has that kind of power,

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:32.400
even though that seems like
a pretty reasonable limit

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.200
on presidential pardon power.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.320
In truth, the Supreme Court
is likely to find that

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.570
that's just a political question,

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:40.670
and that if people aren't
happy with the president

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:44.440
pardoning co-conspirators,
then they can simply

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:47.190
vote him out of office
or the House and Senate

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:50.080
can vote to impeach him out of office

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:52.110
for the act of the pardon itself.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.680
While it's true that the
text does not say impeachment

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.370
and conviction, this
interpretation has never been tried

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.190
in court, and Madison and other
drafters of the Constitution

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:02.160
offered impeachment as a compromise,

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:05.160
a way of checking the
president's executive powers.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.640
But the impeachment process
has been used so sparingly

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:10.900
that we just don't know
exactly what the drafters

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:13.390
of the Constitution thought
and how it should be applied

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.120
in these particular circumstances,

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.730
and it's unlikely that the Supreme Court

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:21.230
is going to seriously curtail
the president's pardon power,

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:22.910
which has been used a lot more frequently

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:24.600
than the impeachment.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.860
So, given that this doesn't sound great,

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.520
is there anything that we can do about it?

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:30.970
The short answer is not really.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.610
The only real check on pardon
power is through impeachment.

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:35.620
The Democrats could argue that Trump's use

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:38.950
of the pardon power is in
this case impeachable itself,

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:41.890
and try to argue that
Republicans in the Senate

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:44.300
should vote to convict
and remove him from power.

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:46.360
But that seems incredibly unlikely.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:49.040
And rather than going through
another round of impeachment,

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:51.980
House Speaker Nancy Pelosi
wants to pass legislation

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:53.760
limiting the president's pardon powers.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.260
- This is an abuse of power
that the president is, again,

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:59.050
trying to manipulate
federal law enforcement

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:01.110
to serve his political interest.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:03.580
- Her proposal tracks the
suggestion of legal scholars

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:05.490
who believe that the word
impeachment should mean

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.200
that the president's co-conspirators

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.390
shouldn't get a pardon or reprieve.

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.440
But if Congress passes a law limiting

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:13.230
the president's constitutional powers,

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:15.550
it would probably be unconstitutional,

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:18.200
if the Supreme Court doesn't believe that

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:21.810
that interpretation of the
pardon clause is accurate.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:24.070
Remember, the Constitution trumps any law

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:25.490
that Congress can pass.

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.960
So, in reality, it would probably require

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.410
a constitutional amendment to change

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.210
the meaning of that text.

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.700
Now, Roger Stone may be a dirty trickster,

00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:35.980
and he may have conspired with WikiLeaks,

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.480
who may have conspired
with the Russian GRU

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:39.840
to destroy our democracy,

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.140
but all of this could have been avoided

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:43.590
if the DNC had used a
little email security.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.060
Now, we should all back up our files,

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:47.180
but to back them up without
security is almost as bad

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:49.640
as asking the Russians to
hack your political opponent.

